Senile Pruritus
Geriatric & Frailty
Intense generalized itching >65 years, Severe diffuse xerosis, Excoriations/lichenification, Absence of primary dermatological rash

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GEF-043
Reason for Visit: Acute sleep deprivation, severe agitation, and bleeding skin lesions secondary to relentless, full-body itching.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an elderly patient suffering from intense, unrelenting generalized pruritus causing severe sleep deprivation over 72 hours, leading to acute psychological distress and bleeding excoriations. Urgent clinical assessment was mandated to rule out acute scabies infestation, severe liver or kidney failure, or adverse drug reactions, providing immediate symptomatic relief and preventing secondary systemic infection prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I am literally tearing my own skin off; the itching is driving me completely insane and I haven't slept in three days."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports intense, generalized, and unrelenting itching primarily affecting their torso and limbs. They explicitly note the complete absence of a primary underlying dermatological rash (like hives or blisters) prior to the onset of the scratching. They describe worsening of the pruritus during the current dry winter months and note it flares aggressively after taking hot showers. The itching is causing severe sleep disruption, resulting in frequent nocturnal awakenings and acute exhaustion tonight. They admit to chronic, repeated scratching, resulting in bleeding excoriations and secondary lichenification (thickened skin). They complain of severe diffuse xerosis, stating their skin feels dry, rough, and "like sandpaper." The intense itching has led to profound psychological distress and agitation.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently severely impaired by exhaustion and acute distress.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of secondary bacterial skin infection (cellulitis/impetigo) from deep scratching; acute psychological crisis from sleep deprivation.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/85 | HR: 90 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Highly agitated, exhausted, actively scratching their arms and chest during the consultation.
Physical Exam: Integumentary exam reveals severe diffuse xerosis (asteatotic skin with fine cracking) universally across the body. There are widespread, linear excoriations with fresh, pinpoint bleeding on the extensor surfaces of the arms, back, and lower legs. Secondary lichenification is present in easily reachable areas. Crucially, there is an absolute absence of a primary dermatological rash (no wheals, no burrows in web spaces, no target lesions). There is no scleral icterus to suggest acute severe liver failure. The skin does not appear overtly infected (no spreading erythema or purulence). (An entirely normal comprehensive metabolic panel confirming the absence of a systemic metabolic cause is an anticipated diagnostic tetrad finding, pending formal lab work).
Environmental Observations: The home is heavily heated with central heating, making the ambient air extremely dry.
Medication Reconciliation: No recent addition of new medications, specifically ruling out acute drug-induced pruritus or new opiates.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Senile Pruritus (Idiopathic Xerotic Pruritus) - Status: Acute Exacerbation with sleep deprivation
Differentials:

Scabies Infestation - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of characteristic burrows in the finger web spaces, wrists, or axillae, and the itching is diffuse rather than localized to specific classical mite areas.

Uraemic or Hepatic Pruritus - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of jaundice, scleral icterus, or profound new oedema, though urgent baseline bloods (LFTs, U&Es) are mandatory to formally exclude these silent metabolic causes.

Acute Allergic Contact Dermatitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The generalized, full-body nature of the itch and the absolute absence of primary erythematous plaques, vesicles, or localized rashes indicating a contact trigger.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (Severe distress and risk of skin infection)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented in a state of acute exhaustion and agitation driven by severe, unrelenting full-body pruritus secondary to profound skin dryness (xerosis). Telephone triage correctly identified the severe psychological distress and risk of self-harm through excoriation. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period would have resulted in another night of absolute sleep deprivation, immense suffering, and likely progression to deep, infected skin wounds. This urgent after-hours intervention allowed for the clinical exclusion of infectious (scabies) or primary inflammatory rashes, facilitated immediate aggressive topical therapy, and provided a short-term oral sedative antihistamine to break the itch-scratch-wake cycle and restore crucial sleep.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed a thick, greasy emollient (e.g., 50% white soft paraffin/50% liquid paraffin ointment) to be applied heavily to the entire body immediately. Prescribed a short course of a first-generation sedating antihistamine (e.g., promethazine 10-25mg at night) strictly to provide sedation and break the nocturnal itch-scratch cycle for the next 3 nights. Ordered comprehensive bloods (FBC, CMP, LFTs, Thyroid) via the GP to definitively rule out systemic causes.
Positive Behavioral Change: Implement a strict "soak and smear" skin hydration routine. Step 1: The patient takes a brief (3-minute), lukewarm (not hot) shower using a soap-free wash, immediately followed by heavily smearing the prescribed thick ointment all over the body while the skin is still damp. Step 2: Upon successfully completing this greasy routine before bed, the patient rewards themselves by putting on loose, soft cotton pajamas (no wool) and having a warm, non-caffeinated drink. Cause-and-effect: The lukewarm water prevents stripping the natural oils, while the immediate application of thick ointment traps the moisture, directly repairing the skin barrier. The soft pajamas and warm drink reinforce this difficult, messy routine by providing immediate physical comfort and preventing nighttime scratching.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Advised keeping fingernails trimmed extremely short to minimize damage from involuntary nocturnal scratching.
Follow-up: Routine GP review required within 3-5 days to review the blood test results and monitor the excoriations.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transport to the Emergency Department if the excoriated skin suddenly becomes bright red, hot, swollen, and increasingly painful (signs of cellulitis), or if the patient develops a high fever with shaking chills.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The physical exam shows severe dry skin is the likely cause of your itching; however, urgent blood tests are required to ensure your liver and kidneys are functioning normally.
Clinical Course: The thick ointment will immediately soothe the skin, and the nighttime tablet will force you to sleep. Healing the broken skin barrier takes weeks of religious, daily ointment application.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Go to the nearest Emergency Department immediately if any of the scratch marks become bright red, hot, swollen, and ooze pus, or if you develop a high fever. You must see your GP later this week to complete the required blood tests.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: As we age, our skin loses its ability to hold onto moisture. The central heating and hot showers have stripped your skin entirely of oil, causing microscopic cracks. Your nerve endings are exposed and firing constantly, causing the unbearable itch. The thick, greasy ointment acts as a fake barrier to protect those nerves and lock moisture in.
Actionable Steps: Stop taking hot showers immediately; use only lukewarm water. Apply the thick ointment heavily while the skin is still damp. Keep your fingernails filed perfectly smooth. Take the nighttime tablet to help you sleep through the itch tonight.

